<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  W3C widget manifest with Tizen's extensions, for the Samsung TV (`.wgt`) build.

  This file is a TEMPLATE, not the exact file that ships. `build.mjs` copies it
  into the staging directory and substitutes `__STREAMIX_API_ORIGIN__` for the
  real API origin that build was configured with (see the `<access>` element
  below) — the origin is a build-time choice (`STREAMIX_API_ORIGIN`, same
  variable `apps/mobile`'s `build:web` reads), so it cannot be hard-coded here
  without this file silently going stale the next time that origin changes.
  The same step fills the widget version (from version.json, three numbers) and
  the Tizen package and application ids (from the brand name — see manifest.mjs),
  so none of them can drift from the rest of the repository.
  Everything else here ships byte-for-byte.

  Researched against Samsung's own current docs and real shipped Tizen TV apps
  rather than guessed — see apps/tizen/README.md for the sources, because the
  shape of this file (attribute names, namespaces, required_version scale) is
  exactly the kind of thing nobody wants to re-research from scratch.
-->
<widget xmlns="http://www.w3.org/ns/widgets"
        xmlns:tizen="http://tizen.org/ns/widgets"
        id="https://streamix.example/streamix"
        version="__STREAMIX_VERSION__"
        viewmodes="maximized">

  <!--
    required_version="5.5" — pinned to the OLDEST Tizen we intend to support,
    the 2020 TV model year, deliberately not a newer number.

    Tizen's TV releases map to Chromium versions (measured against Samsung's own
    published mapping, cross-checked over several sources during this build's
    research — see the README):

      Tizen 5.0  (2019 TVs)  -> Chromium 63
      Tizen 5.5  (2020 TVs)  -> Chromium 69
      Tizen 6.0  (2021 TVs)  -> Chromium 76
      Tizen 6.5  (2022 TVs)  -> Chromium 85
      Tizen 7.0  (2023 TVs)  -> Chromium 94

    `required_version` is a FLOOR: the Tizen store and a real TV's installer both
    refuse to install a widget on any set older than the version declared here,
    even if the app would actually run fine on it. Setting it to a newer number
    than necessary quietly amputates however many TV-years sit below it; setting
    it to an older number than the app can truly run on fails at first launch
    instead of at install, which is a worse place to discover it.

    5.5 (not 5.0, not 6.0) is chosen because it is the oldest year this app's
    JS actually needs. `apps/web/vite.config.ts` already states and enforces a
    Chrome-68-equivalent SYNTAX floor for the shared web bundle (optional
    chaining, nullish coalescing, logical assignment, private fields all need
    Chrome ~80 UNTRANSPILED, so Vite transpiles down to that target — see the
    long comment there). Chromium 69 (Tizen 5.5) sits just above that floor;
    Chromium 63 (Tizen 5.0) does not carry `@layer`-flattened CSS problems any
    worse than 5.5 does (both are pre-`@layer`, both get the same
    `scripts/css-compat.mjs` flattening pass), so 5.0 was a real candidate —
    but it was not chosen because Samsung's own SDK documentation states that
    WebAssembly support (used by `hls.js`'s and `mpegts.js`'s more optimized
    code paths, and a plausible future dependency) starts at Tizen 5.5, and
    because 2019-model TVs are a shrinking, already-old installed base next to
    2020's. If a real support request for a 2019 set ever arrives, the number
    to revisit is this one, not the JS/CSS floors — those already reach lower.
  -->
  <tizen:application
      id="__TIZEN_APP_ID__"
      package="__TIZEN_PACKAGE__"
      required_version="5.5"/>

  <!--
    tv-samsung, not the generic "tv" profile. Verified against real, currently
    shipping Samsung sample apps (e.g. Samsung/TizenTVApps, the Bitmovin Tizen
    TV demo) — both values exist in the wild, but "tv-samsung" is what Samsung's
    own Tizen Studio TV project templates and store submissions use, and it is
    what unlocks the Samsung-specific product APIs (AVPlay among them) that a
    plain "tv" profile does not declare intent to use.
  -->
  <tizen:profile name="tv-samsung"/>

  <name>Streamix</name>
  <icon src="icon.png"/>
  <content src="index.html"/>

  <!--
    W3C Access Requests Policy (WARP): a Tizen web app cannot reach any network
    origin it has not explicitly listed here, `tizen:privilege internet` or not
    — the privilege turns network access ON at all, `access` says WHICH origins.
    `subdomains="true"` because the API origin can differ by build (see
    apiOrigin() in apps/web/vite.config.ts) and some deployments front it
    behind a shared domain's subdomains.

    The placeholder is substituted by build.mjs — see the file header.
  -->
  <access origin="__STREAMIX_API_ORIGIN__" subdomains="true"/>

  <!--
    Internet access itself. Without this the `access` element above is moot —
    WARP and the privilege system are two separate gates and both must open.
  -->
  <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/internet"/>

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT requested yet: `tv.inputdevice`, `tv.audio`,
    `tv.channel`, `tv.window`, `drmplay`. Samsung's review process penalizes
    over-broad privilege requests, and none of those APIs are called by this
    build — AVPlay integration (which would need some of them) is a known open
    item, not yet implemented; see README.md. Add the specific privilege the
    day the code that needs it lands, not before.
  -->

  <tizen:setting
      screen-orientation="landscape"
      context-menu="disable"
      background-support="disable"
      encryption="disable"
      install-location="auto"
      hwkey-event="enable"/>
  <!--
    hwkey-event="enable" is what makes the remote's Return/Back key reach the
    page at all, as a `tizenhwkey` DOM event carrying keyCode 10009 — without
    it the platform swallows that key before JS ever sees it. Whether
    `apps/web/src/lib/remoteKeys.ts` already recognizes 10009 was not checked
    from here: that file is mid-edit in a concurrent session (remote-key
    handling), and apps/tizen/ does not touch anything outside itself. Recorded
    as an open item in README.md rather than assumed either way.

    context-menu="disable" turns off the platform's own long-press/right-click
    menu, which exists for a mouse-and-keyboard web page and has no sensible
    trigger on a D-pad remote; leaving it enabled is how you get a
    browser-chrome context menu stealing focus from the app's own overlays.

    background-support="disable" because this app has no reason to keep
    running once the TV backgrounds it — it is a foreground video player, not
    a widget with ambient behavior, and leaving background support on is a
    battery/CPU cost on a box with neither in the TV sense but with cheap ARM
    that shares thermal budget with everything else running.
  -->

</widget>
